me{sin}⁡x$ is a solution to the original differential equation. \n\nAmong the given options, only $\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x$ is not a solution."} :step/id #uuid "d40ce1a8-b6a7-582c-8f49-39f5f17648ff"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479907 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479843 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10569fcb-7e9f-575e-9509-740ee2036a5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pm 2\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479383} {:answer/id #uuid "273613c1-7c87-51dc-8032-d46e680c50a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1\\pm \\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479416} {:answer/id #uuid "3d4cc1a3-5fc9-57b5-8c0b-aee4588c2693" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\pm 2\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479451} {:answer/id #uuid "5d807d68-7dd1-5e64-a6c9-60f86d530db1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pm \\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479493} {:answer/id #uuid "9f6f7437-a625-52be-82dd-a8fe85b8d19f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pm \\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479602}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479493} :answer-field/id #uuid "cdc5453e-43b0-5db5-af2d-6f25e95cf391" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479806}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "2e82d486-592d-55be-b437-74783b93d164" :question/math-academy-id "q-64691" :question/problem "Consider the equation ${y}^{″}+y=0,$ where $y=y(t).$ If $y={e}^{\\lambda t}$ is a solution, then $\\lambda =$" :question/worked-solution "Assuming $y={e}^{\\lambda t}$ and differentiating $y$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda t},\\,{y}^{″}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda t}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda t}+{e}^{\\lambda t} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda t}({\\lambda }^{2}+1) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+1=0.\n$$\n\nSolving the auxiliary equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+1 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2} & =-1 \\\\ \\lambda  & =\\pm \\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,  $\\lambda =\\pm \\text{i}$ are the (purely imaginary) roots of the auxiliary equation."} :step/id #uuid "ff6cb89c-b94f-558e-a5a4-97618d860b31" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479904}}] :activity/title "Second-Order Homogeneous ODEs: Characteristic Equations With Complex Roots" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479354}]}
